; Phalanx - the heavy gunship (GAMEPLAY-DESIGN A7, roster type EIGHT).
;
; THE ESCALATION ANCHOR. P4.6 says a pressured enemy fields heavier things rather
; than more things, and until this landed `HEAVY_TYPES` was [aegis, screamer] -
; a bomber that correctly leaves defensive garrisons (A.q1) and a jammer with no
; weapon. So "the war escalates" meant "the war gets foggier" and nothing else.
; This is the heavy DEFENDER the roster never had.
;
; THE PAIR THAT IS THE TYPE, read together:
;
;   shield_arc_deg 140    = the screen covers a front arc, not the whole body.
;   shield_slew_deg_s 45  = and it chases whoever is hurting it, at this rate.
;
; That is the whole counterplay and it is POSITIONAL, which is what makes this a
; different enemy from the aegis rather than a bigger one. The aegis's screen
; asks what you are shooting WITH (a damage threshold no chip gun crosses); this
; one asks where you are shooting FROM. Park in one orbit slot and the arc
; catches up and your damage stops landing; keep moving and it lags and your
; damage lands. Meanwhile every surviving mount fires at you wherever you are, so
; moving never makes you SAFE - it decides whether your shots count.
;
; 140 degrees leaves 220 open, so there is always somewhere that works. 45 deg/s
; means a full swing to the far side takes about 4 seconds, which a quad crosses
; comfortably and a heavy frame does not - P4.4's frame pressure, emergent from
; one number rather than authored per frame.
;
; mount_count 3 at mount_hull 30 - evenly spaced, so there is no bearing without
; a gun on it (A7: "no blind side you can simply sit in"). Destructible on the
; user's call (2026-08-07): stripping a mount permanently opens that arc, so a
; long fight visibly degrades it. 30 against a 25-damage bolt is two hits, which
; makes taking a gun off a reward for a burst rather than a second boss.
;
; hull 140 sits between the falx's 26 and the aegis's 240. It is NOT the aegis's
; job: this type's durability is meant to live in its arc and its guns, not in a
; long health bar, or the sponge objection the user raised about bombers applies
; here with less excuse - a gunship you grind down is not doing anything
; interesting while you grind it.
;
; speed 8 / accel 7 / turn_speed_deg 60 - A7 asks for "slow and deliberate, it
; does not chase, it holds ground and denies it". 8 is 0.6x a raider, matching
; the sketch. `turn_speed_deg` is the MOUNT slew here, not the body's: the guns
; track at 60 deg/s, which is what a jinking pilot degrades.
;
; damage 7 x fire_rate 1.2 x 3 mounts = about 25 damage a second with everything
; alive, a quarter of a Kestrel. It has to be a reason to leave, not an instant
; death, because the answer is repositioning and repositioning takes time.
;
; preferred_range 30 is the standoff it wants from what it guards - it slides
; within a 14 m leash to keep the fight at arm's length and never follows.
;
; strength_cost 6 makes it the most expensive unit in the manifest, above the
; aegis's 4. That is deliberate: it is what a node buys INSTEAD of a handful of
; raiders when it is being pressed, which is P4.6's escalation expressed as a
; price rather than as a spawn rule.
;
; NOTHING HERE IS BALANCED. Per H6 these are authored starting points; the
; harness measures the result and the human's hands decide the feel.
;
; ONE THING LAYER 1 CANNOT SEE, stated so nobody quotes it wrong: `Lethality`
; models the shield as a plain pool that always applies, because that is what
; `Health` does. That is exactly right for a pilot inside the arc and pessimistic
; for one outside it, so Layer 1 prices the WORST case. The gap between that and
; the harness is the directional shield, and it is the instrument's output rather
; than an error.

[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="EnemyConfig" load_steps=2 format=3]

